WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.435
-Our first guest tonight is
an Oscar-nominated actor

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:03.970
you know from movies such
as "Zombieland,"

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:05.638
"Now You See Me,"
and "The Social Network."

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:08.107
He stars in FX's
"Fleishman is in Trouble."

00:00:08.107 --> 00:00:09.809
New episodes stream Thursdays
on Hulu.

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:10.843
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.843 --> 00:00:12.745
-Can I get Instagram?
Everyone has it.

00:00:12.745 --> 00:00:13.980
-No.
We've answered this before.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.881
No social media until you're 13.

00:00:15.881 --> 00:00:16.950
-Everyone.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:18.618
-Hanna,
you don't even have a phone.

00:00:18.618 --> 00:00:20.152
It's not good
for kids and anxiety.

00:00:20.152 --> 00:00:21.254
Your brain is still developing,

00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:22.455
alright?
-Dad and I'll talk about it.

00:00:22.455 --> 00:00:24.556
-Excuse me. We've already
talked about it, okay?

00:00:24.556 --> 00:00:26.892
-How about my anxiety
that everybody is always on it

00:00:26.892 --> 00:00:28.094
and leaving me out?

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:29.329
-She has a point.
-Yeah?

00:00:30.129 --> 00:00:31.998
[ Laughter ]

00:00:39.772 --> 00:00:42.008
[ Water runs ]

00:00:42.008 --> 00:00:43.343
-You're an animal.

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:44.644
-Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:44.644 --> 00:00:46.745
our friend Jesse Eisenberg,
everybody.

00:00:46.745 --> 00:00:48.714
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:00:48.714 --> 00:00:51.784
[ Upbeat funk plays ]

00:00:51.784 --> 00:00:58.691 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:01.060
How are you?
-I'm great.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:02.194
Thank you so much.
How are you?

00:01:02.194 --> 00:01:03.263
-I'm wonderful.

00:01:03.263 --> 00:01:04.997
I am enjoying
the show a great deal.

00:01:04.997 --> 00:01:06.899
-Thank you so much.
I had not -- I'm ashamed to say

00:01:06.899 --> 00:01:08.668
I had not read the novel
it's based on,

00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:11.103
which was a book that everybody
spoke very highly of.

00:01:11.103 --> 00:01:12.405
-Yeah.
-Had you read the book

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:13.740
before you took on?

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.842
-I bought the book, you know,
I bought the book.

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:18.277
[ Laughter ]
I had it on like --
I had it on on the app.

00:01:18.277 --> 00:01:19.945
I bought a digital copy
of the book, you know,

00:01:19.945 --> 00:01:21.547
as an environmental gesture.
-Sure, that's [indistinct].

00:01:21.547 --> 00:01:23.483
[ Laughter ]
And greatly appreciated.

00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:24.384
Thank you.
-Yeah, you're welcome.

00:01:24.384 --> 00:01:25.785
[ Laughter ]
-And --

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:27.887
Yeah, but then I had --
Before I read it,

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:29.822
I heard an interview
with the writer,

00:01:29.822 --> 00:01:32.658
who's now a friend,
Taffy Brodesser-Akner, a genius,

00:01:32.658 --> 00:01:34.427
and I heard
an interview with her

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:36.796
and she was talking
about her work as a journalist

00:01:36.796 --> 00:01:38.498
for The New York Times Magazine,

00:01:38.498 --> 00:01:40.900
as a culture reporter,
writing about celebrities.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.536
And, once I heard that,
I decided not to read the book

00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:46.439
because I thought maybe it's
about a celebrity and I,

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:49.676
you know, avoid anything having
to do with celebrity culture

00:01:49.676 --> 00:01:53.079
or the arts, you know, based
on just shame and ambivalence

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:55.949
[ Laughter ]
about my own place in it,
you know.

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:59.085
And so, I didn't read it
and I felt good

00:01:59.085 --> 00:02:00.819
about that decision, you know,
because I thought,

00:02:00.819 --> 00:02:03.489
you know, there's a nobility
in my, you know,

00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:05.391
my aversion to anything having
to do with myself.

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:07.226
[ Laughter ]
And then --

00:02:07.226 --> 00:02:08.594
And then they had
sent me the script

00:02:08.594 --> 00:02:10.362
and I saw that it wasn't
about anybody famous

00:02:10.362 --> 00:02:12.532
and I immediately dove
into the book and I loved it.

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:14.701
-Yeah.
And, for those who don't know,

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:17.370
it's about a divorced father

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.438
who's sort of going back
into the dating scene,

00:02:19.438 --> 00:02:21.541
but then his ex-wife disappears

00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:23.643
and he's left with the children.
-Right.

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:27.614
-One of the elements of this
is he is introduced by his --

00:02:27.614 --> 00:02:29.248
you know, his younger colleagues
introduce him

00:02:29.248 --> 00:02:31.550
to the dating apps,
which he has missed.
-That's right. Yeah.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:34.386
-You also, in real life,
missed the dating apps, right?

00:02:34.386 --> 00:02:35.955
-Yes, thank God.
-You met your wife a while back.

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:37.323
-Met my wife a long time ago.

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:39.758
I, you know, pursued her
for like a year and a half

00:02:39.758 --> 00:02:41.927
before she agreed to date me,
so then --

00:02:41.927 --> 00:02:42.929
-Really?!
-Yes.

00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:44.430
-So, that's a lot harder
than an app.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:45.431
-Yes.
-Yeah.

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:47.099
[ Laughter ]
-Yes, yes, exactly.

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:48.200
I mean, I -- you know,

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:49.334
I went through like, you know,

00:02:49.334 --> 00:02:51.270
the 12 stages of Hercules

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.305
[ Laughter ]
or whatever it's called,
you know, to get her to date me.

00:02:53.305 --> 00:02:54.874
You know, it was
in New York City, though,

00:02:54.874 --> 00:02:56.542
so that just means, you know,
sending her my jokes

00:02:56.542 --> 00:02:58.210
and my scripts
and stuff like that.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:01.681
[ Laughter ]
I didn't have to fight a lion
or anything like that.

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:03.950
But then, you know,
to see the ease

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:05.651
with which people can date,

00:03:05.651 --> 00:03:07.753
you know, of course,
my first thought, you know,

00:03:07.753 --> 00:03:10.289
was like, "I made a huge
mistake," you know, obviously.

00:03:10.289 --> 00:03:11.290
[ Laughter ]

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:12.658
And then you go
through the thing

00:03:12.658 --> 00:03:14.527
and you see everybody on the
yacht or wherever they are

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:16.829
and then you realize,
"No, thank God.

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:18.898
Thank God for that year
and a half of misery."

00:03:18.898 --> 00:03:20.266
[ Laughter ]

00:03:20.266 --> 00:03:22.634
-I missed it as well,
I think, very mercifully.

00:03:22.634 --> 00:03:24.537
But, again, if you see,
you know, younger friends

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:26.239
who are on it, you're like,
"Oh, my God, it's so easy,"

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:28.941
but then you realize there's
just a deep, empty sadness

00:03:28.941 --> 00:03:30.810
in their eyes, you know,
and that makes me happy.
-Yes, yes, yes.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:34.013
[ Laughter ]
I like stability over happiness
any day of the week.

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:35.681
[ Laughter ]
You know what I mean? Yeah.

00:03:35.681 --> 00:03:37.049
-That was your wedding vow,
right?

00:03:37.049 --> 00:03:38.684
[ Laughter ]
-That's what I said to her.

00:03:38.684 --> 00:03:39.718
That's -- Which was it?

00:03:39.718 --> 00:03:40.920
That's why I asked her
to marry me.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.122
I said, "Would you be stable...
[ Laughter ]

00:03:43.122 --> 00:03:46.859
...and with a ceiling
on stability, and nothing else?"

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:48.594
And she said no.

00:03:48.594 --> 00:03:50.796
[ Awws ]
And then I waited for another
period of time and then we --

00:03:50.796 --> 00:03:51.930
-That's great. Yeah.
-Yeah. We were engaged.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:53.999
-People always come
around to stability.

00:03:53.999 --> 00:03:55.268
There is a flashback.

00:03:55.268 --> 00:04:00.472
We see sort of your courtship
with your ex-wife,

00:04:00.472 --> 00:04:01.941
played wonderfully
by Claire Danes.

00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:05.077
And this is --
That's a wig?

00:04:05.077 --> 00:04:07.012
-That's a wig. It's great.
-Yeah, it's a really good,

00:04:07.012 --> 00:04:08.848
believable wig, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:11.184
[ Laughter ]
But, you know, it was like
scripted and decided

00:04:11.184 --> 00:04:13.019
by like a team of people and FX,

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:14.386
you know, network people,
people, you know,

00:04:14.386 --> 00:04:16.689
professional people,
that I should look, you know,

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:19.926
younger and awkward,
you know, and --

00:04:19.926 --> 00:04:23.062
[ Laughter ]

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:24.463
Yeah.

00:04:24.463 --> 00:04:26.966
[ Applause ]
You know. [ Chuckles ]

00:04:26.966 --> 00:04:28.101
[ Laughter ]

00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:31.770
And then they made
like a $10,000 wig

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:34.640
that looked exactly like
how I normally look.

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:36.075
[ Laughter ]

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:37.376
And, you know,
you get the sense that like,

00:04:37.376 --> 00:04:39.244
"Oh, corporate realizes

00:04:39.244 --> 00:04:41.680
that I, Jesse Eisenberg,
in my natural state,

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.983
am awkward and, you know,
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.983 --> 00:04:45.718
young-looking,
just generally speaking,

00:04:45.718 --> 00:04:48.621
and that I'm not presentable
on television, unless it's like,

00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:50.656
you know, qualified that this is
the awkward years."

00:04:50.656 --> 00:04:52.124
[ Laughter ]

00:04:52.124 --> 00:04:54.059
-I have a lot more to ask you.
We'll be right back

00:04:54.059 --> 00:04:56.596
[ Cheering and applause ]
with more from Jesse Eisenberg,
everybody.

